    "teacher_markdown": "**Suggested experiments and what they reveal:**\r\n\r\n- Add a second `take_paint()` right after the first: no visible change to `has_paint()` — still `True` either way. Worth calling out explicitly: `has_paint()` is a yes/no check, not a count, so taking more paint doesn’t make it “more true.”\r\n- Swap `and` → `or` in `ready`: `ready` flips `False → True`, since `has_paint()` alone (`True`) now satisfies `or`. Good contrast with Level 2’s result — same starting conditions, different operator, different answer.\r\n- Swap `or` → `and` in `on_something`: flips `True → False`, since `is_on_bucket()` is `False` at the painting square and `and` needs both sides. Mirror case of the one above — nice pairing for discussion.\r\n- Wrap a `not` around `can_move()` or `is_on_paint()`: flips that one value and cascades into whichever compound expression uses it — reinforces that `not` applies to a single expression before `and`/`or` combine things.\r\n\r\n**Misconceptions to watch for:**\r\n\r\n- Expecting `or` to always produce `True` and `and` to always produce `False` as some kind of rule, rather than depending on the actual values — the paired swap experiments above directly test that.\r\n- After the `take_paint()` experiment, lingering belief that “more paint” should somehow show up in a Boolean — useful moment to name that functions returning `True`/`False` discard magnitude entirely."
